we begin here in germany where the  government has announced it is  activating an early warning plan in  preparation for possible shortages in  gas deliveries from russia economy  minister robert habeck said a crisis  team was being established to closely  monitor gas supplies this follows  moscow's demand for payment and rubles  which germany and other importers have  rejected habek who's from the  environmentalist green party also urged  german industry and households to try  and conserve energy  we  we're in a situation where i have to say  clearly that every kilowatt hour of  energy saved helps  and that is why i would like to combine  the triggering of the early warning  level for gas supplies with the appeal  for health to companies and private  consumers  you are helping germany you are helping  ukraine when you reduce your use of gas  or energy in general  einstein our political correspondent  julia saudelli is with us now outside  the german ministry of economy julia  what is this early warning plan and what  does it mean that they've activated it  so this is part of a three-step  emergency plan for energy  here in germany this uh that was just  activated right now the early warning  alert is the first step of this plan and  what it means in practice is that  the state is not really getting involved  in terms of managing managing energy uh  distribution and and in the country but  it is going to form a crisis team that  is going to be made up of uh the  ministry uh the economy ministry other  authorities including uh the energy  net network agency here in germany but  also energy companies and  this crisis team is mostly going to  monitor the situation here in germany in  terms of of gas  deliveries and gas distribution and is  going to have to deliver regular reports  uh in terms of the situation and uh the  other two uh steps would only be  activated if the gas situation would be  to worsen  to worse and julia germany has  repeatedly said it would not stop  importing energy from russia due to its  dependence how concerned is berlin right  now given this step that moscow may shut  off its gas lines  well what has been made clear is that  there is no reason to panic after this  decision this is just to be prepared in  case of a change in russia's gas  deliveries to germany what the  government is waiting for is this a  deadline set by russian president  vladimir putin he asked his government  the central bank and energy company  gazprom to come up with a plan to  implement the payment in rubles of  russian gas now the g7 governments  including germany have said they will  not pay for the gas and rubles this goes  against the contracts to deliver gas  from russia to germany and so the  government here is waiting to see  what decision might come out of moscow  tomorrow in terms of these gas payments  but  they are not immediately concerned about  um the gas deliveries uh stopping  immediately they're just saying that  they are preparing in case what else did  he say that stood out to you julia  well as we heard in that sound bite he  stressed how it would be really  important for people and companies here  in germany to start reducing their  consumption  obviously that is a big topic and he put  it into the perspective of not only  helping germany at this moment but also  helping ukraine now the government has  said and robert the economy minister  specifically have said that germany  cannot immediately do without russian  gas they are working to  reach a point where they can do without  it in the next few years right now  they're trying to reduce the  the the use of it but it will take time  so it would be important for for now for  germans themselves to take a step and  try to to reduce their consumption  dw correspondent julia saudali reporting  thank you very much  further analysis of this decision to  activate an early warning plan should  there be gas shortages in germany dw  chief international editor richard  walker joins us now richard germany  looks to be caught on the back heel here  a little bit after years of being  criticized particularly the previous  government for their energy policy  yeah that's right and that criticism has  been international but also here in  germany um that  germany simply allowed itself to become  far too dependent on russian sources of  energy oil and gas also coal but  particularly natural gas where as we've  been hearing uh the  the usage of natural gas even exceeded  50  from russia at times and it's quite  interesting  um to watch robert harbeck so the  minister has been announcing this he is  the minister really in the middle of all  of this action about trying to find  different sources of energy because he's  from the green party and he's basically  been saying we told you so about this  dependence on russian gas the green  party of course with its agenda of of  tackling climate change wants to reduce  fossil fuel use altogether but in  particular it's been very very critical  of russia and in fact he was hosting um  an international conference uh yesterday  here in berlin on the transition towards  cleaner forms of energy and he used very  stark language about russia germany's  dependence on russia describing it as  being stupid that germany in hindsight  um  enabled this kind of level of dependency  to to  develop and kind of this this problem  having been on two levels first of all  too much dependency on any one country  and then secondly too much dependency on  this specific country um too much belief  that russia might change um that when  you build an interdependence  with another country that that you  foster a more positive relationship that  this has simply not come to pass and  particularly the fact that the nordstrom  2 gas pipeline which became emblematic  of the debate around germany's  dependence on russia that this was even  built  after russia annexed crimea illegally  back in 2014 i think this is seen as the  really the the greatest emblem of this  failure talk to us a little bit more  about minister habik he was recently in  qatar he was recently in the uae seeking  out new energy deals it seems that  germany has backed itself into a corner  again as he said this was a stupid  decision policy planning with regard to  the previous government and now they're  being forced to turn to these countries  in desperation yeah um but  they have to i mean  the united states has said that it will  it increase supplies of energy to europe  but that's not going to be enough to to  meet the fall short and the full  shortfall um so robert habeck has been  in the middle east visiting countries  which have been criticized very strongly  by his party for their  records on human rights and it's been  interesting to watch  on that tour he was making instagram  videos trying to explain to his um uh to  the german people like his thinking in  doing this and i think he had a couple  of points on the one hand he was saying  well you know still any relationship  with any of these countries is not going  to reach anything like the dependence  that that um the level of dependence  that germany had on russia  but also some of the thinking that  reflects this german attitude to what's  been called vandal deutsche hanel this  attitude that you can kind of change  countries through trading with them some  of that was visible in what he said in  qatar for example where he started  talking about he had been raising issues  of workers rights workers representation  pay and things like that and hoping that  that would change in qatar so some of  that old german thinking does still seem  to be there dw chief international  editor richard walker thank you richard  i'm joined now by rob watts from dw's  business department rob germany has long  been criticized for its reliance on  russian gas now moscow is demanding that  berlin pay in rubles why is moscow  asking for this you've got to bear in  mind that the russian economy is in real  trouble from the sanctions that have  been imposed you know they've got  inflation of over 14 percent uh you've  also got the fact that the ruble has  lost 40 of its value since the start of  the war and that's what this is really  all about now one of the ways that you  stabilize a currency which the russian  government really needs to do  is to increase demand for it and one of  the ways you can increase demand for it  is by forcing people who spend a lot of  money with you to spend rubles with you  so that's one of the things behind what  uh president putin himself because he  announced this himself is is pushing but  also you've got the fact that you know  sanctions uh make it much harder for  russian organizations russian  individuals as well to deal in euros and  dollars if they want to trade them with  other countries because there's  sanctions in place to stop that so again  by getting more rubles in they start to  find a way around those sanctions and  that's actually why you're hearing from  from the germans you're also hearing  from other europeans the g7 that this  would undermine the sanctions if germany  would start paying in rubles  talk to us a little bit about how  concerned berlin is right now that  russia might turn off the pipes  uh well  we we do have this deadline tomorrow so  it feels like something very imminent  but you know this is stage one of three  we've got this which is i suppose an  early warning alert and then you've got  the next stage which is an alert level  but it isn't until the third step that  the german government actually starts to  step in and to start to make policy that  means that households but importantly  businesses have to change how they're  using their gas so most likely to be hit  by that first would be heavy industry  steel makers because households would  ultimately be insulated from this as  much as they could be but we would start  to see real problems with how germany  can export its products and you know we  already have all kinds of supply chain  problems in the global economy so it's  not something that anyone wants to  happen but  we are now starting on the road in that  direction talk to us a little bit more  about households what kind of impact  is going to be you know felt by germans  living here  well i mean  you know the cost of gas in europe has  increased fivefold in the last year and  that's a big way in which germany gets  the energy to households you know 15 of  germany's energy comes from gas around  half of that actually does come from  russia so as a result of that gas prices  go up that means bills go up and we're  already seeing that germans  can't help but notice that their energy  bills are already starting to rise and  we are seeing that have a big impact on  people's mood here i mean there was a  some research published yesterday on  consumer confidence here in germany and  it's fallen through the floor in the  last month despite the fact that we've  seen coronavirus restrictions being  relaxed that should have been something  that provided a boost and things should  be looking up in germany but actually  people are starting to be pessimistic  not optimistic  dw businesses rob watts thank you very  much for your insights rob

